<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/foundational/verification-before-publication">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Imagine two people standing on a street corner telling you opposite stories about a car
    crash that happened there. You have no special tools — only questions. Whom did you see?
    Were you facing the road or your phone? Have you told this story to anyone else before me?
    Asking those questions well <em>is</em> journalism. In a world where anyone can publish a
    claim in three seconds, the newsroom's product is not the claim — it is the chain of checks
    behind the claim. Anyone can be a publisher; a professional is someone whose assertions come
    with an audit trail. That is why journalism's entire value in an infinite-content world
    reduces to one verb: <strong>verified</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This matters because of a quirk of human psychology: information does not need to be true to
    be useful to the person spreading it. A rumour can get a click, sell a product or win an
    election regardless of its accuracy. The only durable defence is a discipline applied
    <em>before</em> publication — because a correction afterwards, however prominent, rarely
    reaches everyone the original claim reached. Think of verification as the cooking, not the
    garnish: once a false story is served, you cannot un-serve it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This lesson walks the newsroom's method end to end — how stories are found and judged, how
    claims are confirmed, how interviews extract and test information, how longer features
    package it, and how sub-editors and editors act as the final gate before print. It doubles
    as a citizen's toolkit: once you know what verification looks like, you can check whether it
    happened to anything you read.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the five checks every claim must survive" widget="equationStep" config={verifyCfg} />

  <h2>What is news: finding and assessing stories</h2>
  <p>
    Before anything can be verified, someone must decide it is worth verifying. Editors use a
    loose but real checklist of news values: <strong>impact</strong> (how many people, how
    deeply), <strong>proximity</strong> (near you in space or concern), <strong>prominence</strong>
    (famous names attract attention), <strong>timeliness</strong> (why print it today),
    <strong>conflict</strong>, <strong>novelty</strong> (the classic "man bites dog"), and
    <strong>human interest</strong>. A curriculum change affecting 40,000 pupils usually
    outranks a celebrity haircut, even though the haircut might get more clicks — and the
    tension between "important" and "attention-grabbing" is a daily editorial negotiation, not
    a solved problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Stories come from three broad currents. <strong>Enterprise</strong> stories come from the
    reporter's own ideas: reading a council agenda, noticing a pattern, following up a tip.
    <strong>Event</strong> stories come from scheduled happenings: courts, parliaments, press
    conferences, sports. <strong>Reactive</strong> stories respond to the world: a flood, a
    leak, a viral video. Crucially, sources talk to journalists for a reason — politicians want
    coverage, companies want favour, whistle-blowers want change. Assessing a story therefore
    includes assessing the <em>storyteller's motive</em>: who benefits from this being
    published, and what does this source get out of it?
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> many people assume journalists simply report "the facts".
    Selection is unavoidable. Out of thousands of events each day, a newsroom prints twenty.
    The professional duty is not to have no perspective — that is impossible — but to keep the
    <em>facts</em> and the <em>viewpoint</em> in separate, clearly labelled rooms.
  </p>

  <h2>News writing and reporting: the inverted pyramid</h2>
  <p>
    The dominant news form is the inverted pyramid: the most important information first — who,
    what, where, when, why and how — followed by supporting details, then background. It was
    reportedly forged on the Civil War telegraph wires, where a transmission could die at any
    moment and the dispatch had to carry its cargo in descending order of importance. That
    design still fits the modern web perfectly: readers skim, editors cut from the bottom, and
    search engines weight the opening lines.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pyramid is also a verification instrument. Writing "the most newsworthy fact first"
    forces the reporter to rank claims by confidence and consequence, and ranking exposes
    weakness. If the lead must say "police said a fire killed three people", that attribution
    signals the claim's provenance: police are the source, death tolls in the first hours are
    provisional, and a reporter who writes "three people died in a fire" flatly has borrowed
    more certainty than they own. Good attribution is honesty about knowledge — "according to a
    person briefed on the matter" tells you exactly how strong the ground is, and that the
    journalist is telling the truth about their footing.
  </p>
  <p>
    The related genre boundary matters for verification too. <strong>News</strong> reports what
    happened and is verifiable. <strong>Opinion</strong> argues from values and is labelled.
    <strong>Analysis</strong> explains why, using interpretation that is disclosed. A claim
    verified to appear in news can be quoted; an opinion can be quoted <em>as an opinion</em>
    but never laundered into a fact. Confusing the three is how trust dies.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> readers sometimes think the pyramid exists "for people who
    skim". That is a side benefit, not the point. It exists because writing the ending first is
    a truth test: you cannot lead with a claim you have not verified, and the discipline of the
    lead is what catches unverified claims before they reach page two.
  </p>

  <h2>Interviewing and sourcing: the human side of checking</h2>
  <p>
    Most of what journalism knows enters through interviews, and every interview is
    simultaneously a data-collection exercise and a verification exercise. The craft rules exist
    to keep both honest. Prepare so thoroughly you can recognise a wrong answer. Ask open
    questions — who, what, why, how — and then shut up; silence is the most reliable follow-up
    question ever invented. Keep questions short, one at a time, and end with "what have I not
    asked that I should have?" Interviewees rarely volunteer damaging context unprompted, but
    many will share it if invited.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interviews come in flavours with different verification weights. An <strong>on-the-record</strong>
    quote can be published with attribution. <strong>On background</strong> ("a defence
    official") shapes the story without naming its origin. <strong>Off the record</strong>
    information cannot be published from that source at all — it can only point where to look.
    A professional keeps these states straight, because a slipped agreement destroys a source's
    career and the journalist's access. Anonymity is a bargain granted only when the information
    is vital, unobtainable on the record, and the editor knows who the source is; a paper that
    grants anonymity freely is selling its credibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two-source rule lives here. One source can be wrong, biased or playing you; the second
    source must be genuinely <em>independent</em> — not a colleague who heard the same briefing,
    not the spokesperson of the same department. Two eyewitnesses to the same crash count less
    than one eyewitness plus the traffic-camera file. Verification is triangulation: each
    independent line of evidence should reach the claim from a different direction.
  </p>

  <h2>Feature writing: the long form</h2>
  <p>
    Not everything is a 300-word wire piece. A <strong>feature</strong> is the longer,
    free-form sibling of news: it can open with a scene, follow a person, explain a system or
    investigate an institution. Features still carry the verification burden — often a heavier
    one, because a feature stakes narrative authority rather than the speed authority of news.
    A news report claims "this happened"; a feature claims "I understand this", and readers
    forgive a slow start but not a fabricated detail.
  </p>
  <p>
    Features have their own sub-genres with different verification loads. A
    <strong>profile</strong> needs multiple interviews plus the subject's paper trail. An
    <strong>explainer</strong> needs expert review: the journalist's understanding must survive
    a specialist reading it. An <strong>investigation</strong> is essentially one long
    verification exercise — documents, data, interviews and legal review converging before a
    word is published, because an investigative story that misses can end in a libel judgment.
    The writing craft — scene, character, structure — is real work, but every scene must be
    sourced. "Reconstructed dialogue" that nobody attests to is fiction wearing journalism's
    clothes.
  </p>

  <h2>Sub-editing and editorial decision-making: the last gate</h2>
  <p>
    Before publication, copy passes through the <strong>sub-editor</strong>, whose job is far
    more than fixing typos. Subs check facts against the story's own sourcing, cut to fit,
    write headlines that the body text can support, check legal risk (defamation, contempt,
    privacy), and apply the style guide for consistency. A headline is a claim too: a body that
    says "police are investigating whether…" cannot honestly sit under a headline saying
    "minister stole funds". Clickbait mismatches are not just taste failures; they are
    verification failures.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above the subs sit editors making the publish-or-hold decision. The classic calculus weighs
    confidence, consequence and speed. Publishing a wrong report of a death is a catastrophic,
    irreversible harm; holding a true exclusive for an hour costs little. When a story is
    competitive and unconfirmed, the professional move is to publish what you <em>can</em>
    stand behind — "a powerful explosion was heard; authorities have not confirmed casualties"
    — rather than the unconfirmed claim itself. Editing is the institutional memory of these
    trade-offs, and a good editor is the person empowered to say no to the newsroom's own
    enthusiasm.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a correction means the paper failed." Actually, the opposite
    signal is usually true. Every serious newsroom errs; the trustworthy ones correct visibly,
    explain what went wrong, and log the correction prominently. A site with no corrections is
    not a site that is always right — it is a site that never tells you when it was wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two deeper threads are worth pulling. The first is <strong>economics</strong>: verification
    is expensive — a single investigative story can consume months of a reporter's salary —
    while a rumour is free to publish. This asymmetry explains the modern information disorder:
    rumour outruns truth for the same reason counterfeit money outruns the mint. Structural
    responses exist — wire services as shared verification infrastructure, news agencies'
    fact-check desks, independent fact-checking organisations, and provenance standards like
    C2PA, which cryptographically attaches capture data to images so a "real" photo carries
    its own receipt.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second is <strong>prebunking and literacy</strong>. Research on misinformation suggests
    that corrections after the fact are less effective than inoculation before it: teaching
    people to recognise the standard manipulation techniques (fear, outrage, false urgency)
    measurably reduces susceptibility. The reader's toolkit mirrors the newsroom's: find the
    original source; check whether two independent outlets confirm it; ask who benefits; look
    at what is not mentioned; and imagine what a correction would look like if this were wrong.
    The strongest defence against misinformation is not scepticism of everything — it is
    knowing what verification looks like and checking whether it happened.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are honest open problems here too. Generative AI makes fabricated text, images and
    video cheap and fluent, which raises the value of document-based and on-the-ground
    verification while devaluing "it looked real" as a test. And the two-source rule itself has
    limits: two sources can be wrong the same way when they share a defective origin — a trap
    that contributed to famous intelligence failures on Iraqi weapons before 2003. Verification
    is a discipline, not a spell; it lowers the probability of error, and honest journalism says
    so.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> SPJ Code of Ethics; Kovach &amp; Rosenstiel, The Elements of Journalism; NCTJ Essential Journalism syllabus; BBC and AP verification guidelines; C2PA provenance specification; prebunking research (Roozenbeek &amp; van der Linden).</p>
